3. Customer Error-Handler Tests

Created customer exception-handler tests covering:

CustomerNotFoundException -> 404 + CUS-001.

AddressNotFoundException -> 404 + CUS-005.

DuplicateCustomerException -> 409 + CUS-003.

IllegalArgumentException -> 400 + VAL-002.

Unexpected exception -> 500 + SYS-001.

Request path and timestamp are populated where applicable.

4. Account Error-Handler Tests

AccountNotFoundException -> 404 + ACC-001.

CustomerServiceUnavailableException -> 503 + DEP-001.

IllegalArgumentException -> 400 + VAL-002.

Unexpected exception -> 500 + SYS-001.

The dependency failure test protects the service-to-service outage contract.

5. Why Handler Tests Matter

Existing service tests can still pass even if a developer accidentally maps an exception to the wrong API error code. Direct handler tests protect the mapping between exception type, HTTP status, stable error code, message, path, and timestamp.

6. Customer Controller MockMvc Tests

Invalid POST /api/v1/customers request -> HTTP 400 + VAL-001.

Validation failure is handled through the real controller/advice HTTP pipeline.

verifyNoInteractions(customerService) proves invalid input is rejected before business logic executes.

Valid POST request -> CustomerService mock invoked -> HTTP 201 Created.

Successful ApiResponse fields and returned CustomerResponse data are asserted.

7. Account Controller MockMvc Tests

Invalid POST /api/v1/accounts request -> HTTP 400 + VAL-001.

Valid account request -> AccountService mock invoked -> HTTP 201 Created.

Response assertions cover accountId, accountNumber, customerId, accountType, balance, and status.

No Oracle database is required because the service layer is mocked.

